    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, you want to know about expense ratios? Well, that's where the goblins really come into play, because they are notorious for eating up all the profits before the investor even gets to see them!\n\nThink of an **ETF** like a goblin who is very efficient but has a tiny, hungry stomach. They operate with low overhead, often tracking an index like a goblin following a very specific, unchangeable trail of breadcrumbs. Because they don't need a fancy office or a team of goblins constantly arguing over which berry to pick, their **expense ratios are usually quite low**. It's like a goblin who just runs around and collects the shiny things without asking for a cut of the pie.\n\nOn the other hand, a **Mutual Fund** is more like a goblin who runs a massive, chaotic banquet. They have a dedicated \"Goblin Manager\" (the portfolio manager) who is constantly making decisions, sometimes changing their mind, and sometimes getting into arguments with other goblins about the best strategy. This requires a lot of resources, a big office, and a lot of goblin labor. Consequently, their **expense ratios are typically higher**. You are essentially paying a premium to feed the g",
